            <ArticleTitle>Sleep deprivation sensitizes human craniofacial muscles.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>116-122</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/08990220.2017.1317241</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>It is unknown whether and how sleep deprivation influences craniofacial muscle sensitivity in healthy humans. We investigated whether total sleep deprivation (TSD) and one night of recovery sleep (RS) can alter mechanical pain sensitivity in temporal and masseter muscles. Fifteen healthy volunteers participated in three consecutive sessions. Pressure pain thresholds were measured on the temporal and masseter muscles. Both temporal and masseter muscles became sensitized after 24 h of TSD. RS reversed the muscle sensitization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
